{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Correction to my previous comment — two claims in it are WRONG\n\nI checked the container logs before acting on either claim, and both fail. Correcting them here because they are wrong in the durable record and would send the next reader after non-defects.\n\n### WRONG #1: \"`|| echo` swallows git's real error\"\n\nIt does not. `cmd || echo msg` *adds* a line on failure; it never suppresses the failing command's stderr. The real error was in the log the whole time:\n\n```\n$ docker logs rebar-mcp-3118334705d2-8093\nfatal: destination path '/var/gerrit/site/mcp-tickets' already exists and is not an empty directory.\nmcp: tickets store clone deferred (deploy canary)\n```\n\nThe diagnostic information was available and legible. Nobody read that far — including me, earlier in this session. That is an operator/attention failure, not a code defect, and there is nothing to fix here.\n\n### WRONG #2: \"ensure runs unconditionally after a failed clone and poisons the directory\"\n\nIt does not poison anything. `reviewbot-ensure-tickets.sh` detects the missing clone and declines:\n\n```\nreviewbot-ensure-tickets: /var/gerrit/site/mcp-tickets is not a git clone yet (clone deferred); skipping\n```\n\nSo my proposed \"don't run ensure after a failed clone\" fix addresses a defect that does not exist. **I have not written it.**\n\n### Where the marker files actually came from — this ticket's stated mechanism is RIGHT\n\n`.ensure-applied` is present in the poisoned dir, so ensure *did* run successfully at some earlier point, which means a working `.git` existed then. `.gitattributes` and `.gitignore` are tracked files on the `tickets` branch, i.e. checked-out working-tree content. So the sequence is: a clone checked out files and ensure converged them, and the `.git` directory later disappeared — a container killed mid-operation, leaving the working tree behind.\n\nThat is exactly what this ticket says: *\"a container removed mid-clone ... leaves a PERSISTENT volume\"*. My \"different path in, same trap\" framing was wrong. The 120s health-gate rollback removing containers mid-clone (and, on at least one occasion this session, my own `docker kill`) is the entry path, as filed.\n\nThe variant detail is still worth a test: the residue observed in production was **no `.git` at all**, not a `.git` with HEAD dangling at `refs/heads/.invalid`. Both are caught by the `rev-parse --verify -q HEAD` gate, so change 2244 is correct **and sufficient as written** — I am not extending it.\n\n### What still stands from the previous comment\n\nUnchanged and independently verified:\n\n- Arm A / Arm B experiment: clone into empty dir → rc=0 (153,628 files); into non-empty dir → `fatal: ... not an empty directory`. Auth/network/PAT/branch all healthy.\n- The live store had no resolvable HEAD and no `.git`, so every re-clone failed and reads returned a **false empty** (`rebar.list_tickets() -> count=0`) on the deployed server.\n- The `store_uninitialized` guard did **not** fire, because `os.path.isdir()` is satisfied by a directory of junk. My earlier report that the guard had fixed false-empty reads in production was wrong; it closes \"directory absent\" only. This ticket already notes that limitation and scopes the remedy to the entrypoint, so I am not filing a competing ticket or widening that predicate.\n- Repair applied and verified: cleared, re-cloned (`HEAD=73ad5058bf`, branch `tickets`), **2763 tickets visible**, `/health` → `{\"in_flight\":0}` behind nginx upstream `127.0.0.1:8093`, and a live read from an external MCP client now returns real ticket data."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787763169397851999,"uuid":"16736da7-48cb-49e3-a128-ee15503ecc83"}